               <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{append}(\tau,({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>append</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{append}(\tau,({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}}))</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l13.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> appending chunk to trace
  </span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{append}(R,\,\mathcal{R}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>append</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{append}(R,\,\mathcal{R}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l14.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> trace reward
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{append}(T,\,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>append</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{append}(T,\,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Estimate Advantages:</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{A}[i]\}_{i=1}^{G}\leftarrow\mathrm{ComputeAdvantage}\!\left(\{R[i]\}_{i=1}^{G}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:8.669em;" width="8.669em"><mi>ComputeAdvantage</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{A}[i]\}_{i=1}^{G}\leftarrow\mathrm{ComputeAdvantage}\!\left(\{R[i]\}_{i=1}^{G}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l17.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> off-the-shelf advantage estimator
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Updating Parameters:</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\leftarrow\frac{1}{G}\sum_{\tau_{g}}\frac{1}{\ell(\tau_{g})}\sum_{l=1}^{|\tau_{g}|}\mathcal{U}({\bm{x}}_{l},{\bm{y}}_{l};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\leftarrow\frac{1}{G}\sum_{\tau_{g}}\frac{1}{\ell(\tau_{g})}\sum_{l=1}^{|\tau_{g}|}\mathcal{U}({\bm{x}}_{l},{\bm{y}}_{l};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l19.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Compute the loss according to <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2 Policy Gradient ‣ 4 Delethink ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\leftarrow\theta+\eta\,\nabla_{\theta}\,J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\leftarrow\theta+\eta\,\nabla_{\theta}\,J</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Making Room for Extra Thinking</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work has tried to shorten reasoning traces at inference time. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2025)</a></cite> drops irrelevant tokens and redundant steps using an extra judge LLM. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2025)</a></cite> prunes low-value segments by perplexity. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al. (2025)</a></cite> iteratively summarizes current reasoning so later steps continue depending only on the summary. InftyThink’s style is fixed by design, distilled from a hand-crafted dataset that locks the model into one pattern. In contrast, Delethink is an RL training approach that learns native Markovian Thinking through RL.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Linear Architectures</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sliding or sparse attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beltagy et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and kernel-based linear attention and low-rank linear attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Katharopoulos et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choromanski et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>)</cite> avoid all-pairs interactions, the quadratic component of attention, by approximations. Mamba architecture replaces self-attention with state-space models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu &amp; Dao, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao &amp; Gu, 2024</a>)</cite>, achieving constant-memory, linear-time generation via recurrent state updates. Despite progress in this field, all state-of-the-art models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemini Team, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> include self-attention even the hybrid systems only interleave self-attention with alternatives. All scale asymptotically quadratic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lieber et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI21 Labs, 2024</a>)</cite>. Delethink does not modify the architecture and is not quadratic in context. It decouples reasoning length and context length, avoiding the quadratic cost. However, Delethink shows continuing reasoning effectively with constant number of tokens is possible, providing evidence that these methods might be more successful in reasoning than other areas.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background</h3>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the RLVR formulation, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazemnejad et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
In this setup, the policy <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is a reasoning language model that, given a prompt <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}=[x_{1},\ldots,x_{N}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}=[x_{1},\ldots,x_{N}]</annotation></semantics></math> (which may include the system prompt, chat history, and a user query), generates a response <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}=[y_{1},\ldots,y_{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}=[y_{1},\ldots,y_{T}]</annotation></semantics></math> autoregressively: <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{y}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The goal of RL training is to maximize expected return while regularizing the policy via a KL penalty against a reference model.
Formally, the objective is</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}\sim\mathcal{D},{\bm{y}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathcal{R}({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}})\right]-\beta\,\mathrm{KL}[\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\text{ref}}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}\sim\mathcal{D},{\bm{y}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathcal{R}({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}})\right]-\beta\,\mathrm{KL}[\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\text{ref}}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dataset of queries, <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reference policy, <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is the KL coefficient, and <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a trajectory-level reward function (verifiable rewards in our case).
The policy <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically initialized from <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized by sampling trajectories and applying policy-gradient updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
