<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Ptx1" class="ltx_part">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_part">Appendix</h2>

<section id="A0.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Table of Content</h4>

<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Deriving Delethink Loss</h3>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that the Delethink Training loss is basically the policy gradient and derive it from scratch. This proof will be trivial to readers familiar with policy gradients (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton et al. (1999)</a></cite>). We include it for completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The RL objective is to maximize the expected return which is the following:</p>
<table id="A1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\left[\mathcal{R}(\tau)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\left[\mathcal{R}(\tau)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Policy gradient is the gradient of the expected return with respect to the policy parameters. That is:</p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\left[\mathcal{R}(\tau)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\left[\mathcal{R}(\tau)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">First, note that the gradient cannot pass through the expectations as we have distributional dependency. That is, the distribution of <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> depends on <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the gradient can pass through the expectation over the queries as the distribution of dataset does not depend on <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="A1.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">As noted, the distribution of <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, the gradient passes through this expectation:</p>
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\nabla_{\theta}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\nabla_{\theta}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Next, the gradient passes through the integration as the bounds of the integration does not depend on <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">However, the term insides the integral is intractable as it is an integration over all possible traces. In order to write this as an expectation so we can estimate this via samples we divide and multiply <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A1.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\frac{\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)}{\pi_{\theta}(\tau)}\mathcal{R}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\frac{\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)}{\pi_{\theta}(\tau)}\mathcal{R}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.5" class="ltx_p">However, the term inside the integral is the derivative of the log probability with respect to the parameters, famously known as the log-trick. That is, <math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)}{\pi_{\theta}(\tau)}=\nabla_{\theta}\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)}{\pi_{\theta}(\tau)}=\nabla_{\theta}\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we have:</p>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A1.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\nabla_{\theta}\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))\mathcal{R}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D}}\int_{\tau\in\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\nabla_{\theta}\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))\mathcal{R}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E10Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(\tau)\mathcal{R}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.6" class="ltx_p">Note that, this is the famous REINFORCE (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams (1992)</a></cite>). Next, we expand <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math>. We know the Delethink trace <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of chunks. That is, <math id="A1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\;=\;\left[\,({\bm{x}}_{1},{\bm{y}}_{1}),\ldots,({\bm{x}}_{L},{\bm{y}}_{L})\,\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\;=\;\left[\,({\bm{x}}_{1},{\bm{y}}_{1}),\ldots,({\bm{x}}_{L},{\bm{y}}_{L})\,\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we can write the log probability of generating <math id="A1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> as the sum of log probabilities of generating each chunk. That is,</p>
<table id="A1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))=\sum_{l=1}^{L}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))=\sum_{l=1}^{L}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.7" class="ltx_p">And if we expand the log probability of generating each chunk into sum of log probability of generating the response tokens:</p>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))=\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t}\mid x_{l},{\bm{y}}_{:t-1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\pi_{\theta}(\tau))=\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t}\mid x_{l},{\bm{y}}_{:t-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.8" class="ltx_p">By substituting this to <a href="#A1.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Appendix A Deriving Delethink Loss ‣ Appendix ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, we get the following:</p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})\mathcal{R}(\tau)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})\mathcal{R}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.9" class="ltx_p">Next, according to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton et al. (1999)</a></cite>, instead of the reward, we can multiply the log probability of each token with its corresponding advantage:</p>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})\hat{A}_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})\hat{A}_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.10" class="ltx_p">This concludes the derivation of the policy gradient form of the objective. However, we will proceed to add PPO clipping, KL loss, and the normalization. They bias the gradient, so the term is not the exact policy gradient anymore. First, we add the PPO clippings:</p>
<table id="A1.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\min\left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}\hat{A}_{t},\;\text{clip}\!\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\hat{A}_{t}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mtext>clip</mtext></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\min\left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}\hat{A}_{t},\;\text{clip}\!\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\hat{A}_{t}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.11" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we add the normalization by the total number of response tokens in the trace <math id="A1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\tau_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\tau_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\frac{1}{\ell(\tau_{g})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\min\left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}\hat{A}_{t},\;\text{clip}\!\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\hat{A}_{t}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mtext>clip</mtext></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\frac{1}{\ell(\tau_{g})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\min\left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}\hat{A}_{t},\;\text{clip}\!\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\hat{A}_{t}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.12" class="ltx_p">Finally, we add the KL term that limits deviation from the reference policy.</p>
<table id="A1.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\frac{1}{\ell(\tau_{g})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\min\left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}\hat{A}_{t},\;\text{clip}\!\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\hat{A}_{t}\right]-\beta\,\mathrm{KL}\!\left[\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mtext>clip</mtext></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{q}}\sim\mathcal{D},\tau\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|{\bm{q}})}\frac{1}{\ell(\tau_{g})}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{t=1}^{|{\bm{y}}_{l}|}\min\left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}\hat{A}_{t},\;\text{clip}\!\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}({\bm{y}}_{l,t})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\hat{A}_{t}\right]-\beta\,\mathrm{KL}\!\left[\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.13" class="ltx_p">Note that this is basically our objective.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Deriving Throughput Relation</h3>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Consider a GPU with maximum memory <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, serving an infinite stream of incoming requests and an equally unbounded stream of completed outputs. Let <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the equilibrium number of concurrent requests. Each request has <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> prefill tokens and <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> decode tokens. In steady state, the KV-cache memory required is</p>
<table id="A2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{*}\;=\;n^{*}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{*}\;=\;n^{*}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">According to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al. (2025)</a></cite>, the equilibrium throughput of an attention-based LLM under a memory constraint is</p>
<table id="A2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{*}\;=\;\frac{n^{*}}{\,d_{0}\;+\;d_{1}\,n^{*}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{*}\;=\;\frac{n^{*}}{\,d_{0}\;+\;d_{1}\,n^{*}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the fixed per-batch overhead and <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the time cost per unit of memory. Thus, throughput exhibits an inverse dependence on the total effective context length. In regimes where
<math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}\,n^{*}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right)\gg d_{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≫</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}\,n^{*}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right)\gg d_{0},</annotation></semantics></math>
we obtain the approximation</p>
<table id="A2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Throughput}^{*}\;\approx\;\frac{n^{*}}{\,d_{1}\,n^{*}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right)}\;=\;\frac{1}{\,d_{1}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>Throughput</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Throughput}^{*}\;\approx\;\frac{n^{*}}{\,d_{1}\,n^{*}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right)}\;=\;\frac{1}{\,d_{1}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}}{2}\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, for the same GPU, the ratio of throughputs when decoding lengths are <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\prime}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\prime}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\prime}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\prime}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathcal{T}_{l^{\prime}_{1}}}{\mathcal{T}_{l^{\prime}_{2}}}\;\approx\;\frac{\frac{1}{d_{1}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}_{1}}{2}\right)}}{\frac{1}{d_{1}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}_{2}}{2}\right)}}\;=\;\frac{\,l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}_{2}}{2}\,}{\,l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}_{1}}{2}\,}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mfrac><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathcal{T}_{l^{\prime}_{1}}}{\mathcal{T}_{l^{\prime}_{2}}}\;\approx\;\frac{\frac{1}{d_{1}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}_{1}}{2}\right)}}{\frac{1}{d_{1}\!\left(l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}_{2}}{2}\right)}}\;=\;\frac{\,l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}_{2}}{2}\,}{\,l+\tfrac{l^{\prime}_{1}}{2}\,}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In the long-thinking regime where <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\prime}\gg l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≫</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\prime}\gg l</annotation></semantics></math>, this simplifies to</p>
<table id="A2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathcal{T}_{l^{\prime}_{1}}}{\mathcal{T}_{l^{\prime}_{2}}}\;\approx\;\frac{l^{\prime}_{2}}{l^{\prime}_{1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathcal{T}_{l^{\prime}_{1}}}{\mathcal{T}_{l^{\prime}_{2}}}\;\approx\;\frac{l^{\prime}_{2}}{l^{\prime}_{1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">establishing that throughput is (approximately) inversely proportional to the decoding length in this regime.  <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Intuition.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Decoding repeatedly accesses the KV cache, whose size, and therefore memory-time cost, grows linearly with the number of decoded tokens <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This linear growth drives the inverse relationship between throughput and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in the long-thinking regime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Delethink FLOPs Crossover vs. LongCoT 24K</h3>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can compute, in closed form, the FLOPs required for reasoning of a certain length under Delethink and LongCoT. We calculated this for our main experiment setup, where Delethink runs with <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{K},m=4\text{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>K</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{K},m=4\text{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, single step with 1000 episodes, and results are shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3 Background ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We highlight the crossover in <a href="#A3.F1" title="In Appendix C Delethink FLOPs Crossover vs. LongCoT 24K ‣ Appendix ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a> at short sequences. Up to roughly <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\text{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>K</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\text{K}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, Delethink spends slightly more FLOPs than LongCoT. That seems counterintuitive: Delethink scales linearly, LongCoT quadratically. This is because in Delethink each token is generated once and then reprocessed as input to the next chunk. While the quadratic compute of attention quickly overtakes this double-counting cost in longer sequences, it matters at short lengths. This is clear when considering non-attention layers. FLOPs in non-attention layers scale linearly, and in Delethink each token is generated once and then reprocessed in the next chunk as a prompt. Therefore, the cost of non-attention layers doubles. The crossover occurs around <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\text{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>K</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\text{K}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in our setting. Beyond this point, Delethink is linearly more efficient in terms of FLOPs. Note that while total backward time tracks total FLOPs, generation time at these response lengths is still slower for LongCoT. This is because generation throughput is limited by memory-access and memory-footprint bottlenecks, not just FLOPs. As explained in <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Empirical Compute ‣ 6 Results ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>, our training still runs faster even in this regime due to faster generation. Our next step, Delethink 96K, is significantly faster than its counterpart LongCoT-RL 96K in both backward pass and generation, to the point that we could not train the latter under our compute resources.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06557v1/flops_vs_seq_len_deepseek_r1_qwen_1_5b_crossover.svg" id="A3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:176/160;" width="176" height="160"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure C.1</span>: </span><span id="A3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">When training R1DistillQwen1.5B with Delethink with <math id="A3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> thinking context, for thinking lengths <math id="A3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 30\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 30\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the number of FLOPs for both is equal. This is because the non-attention components like dense layers dominate here. However, after <math id="A3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> quadratic cost of attention dominates.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="A3.F2.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_python ltx_lstlisting ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="width:338.1pt;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_bold">import</span><span id="lstnumberx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">torch</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_bold">from</span><span id="lstnumberx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">transformers</span><span id="lstnumberx2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_bold">import</span><span id="lstnumberx2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">AutoTokenizer</span>,<span id="lstnumberx2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">AutoModelForCausalLM</span>
