<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Markovian State Size Ablation</h3>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this ablation we vary the size of the Markovian state <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> while holding the per-chunk thinking context fixed at <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and keeping the overall token budget constant. We sweep <math id="A5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{1\text{k},2\text{k},3\text{k},4\text{k},5\text{k},6\text{k},7\text{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{1\text{k},2\text{k},3\text{k},4\text{k},5\text{k},6\text{k},7\text{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure a matched budget across settings, we adjust the Delethink iteration cap <math id="A5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> using the identity <math id="A5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}=\mathcal{C}+(\mathcal{I}-1)(\mathcal{C}-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}=\mathcal{C}+(\mathcal{I}-1)(\mathcal{C}-m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, we set <math id="A5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=\{6,7,8,10,13,19,37\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>19</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=\{6,7,8,10,13,19,37\}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\{1\text{k},2\text{k},3\text{k},4\text{k},5\text{k},6\text{k},7\text{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\{1\text{k},2\text{k},3\text{k},4\text{k},5\text{k},6\text{k},7\text{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We evaluate R1–Distill models (1.5B, 7B, 14B) and Qwen3 30B-A3B on AIME’24, AIME’25, GPQA–Diamond, and LiveCodeBench under identical decoding and verification. Results are summarized in Figure <a href="#A5.F3" title="Figure E.3 ‣ Appendix E Markovian State Size Ablation ‣ Appendix ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Surprisingly, for the R1–Distill models the final accuracy is essentially flat across the entire range of <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, Given <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, shrinking the carried state from <math id="A5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> down to <math id="A5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has negligible effect on all tasks. In contrast, Qwen3 30B-A3B exhibits a marked dependence on <math id="A5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> for AIME’24, AIME’25, and LiveCodeBench, with larger states yielding higher accuracy. This difference aligns with two observations. First, Qwen3 is trained with a substantially larger native context window <math id="A5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> compared to <math id="A5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for the R1 models. Second, the average Delethink trace lengths on AIME and LiveCode are considerably longer than on GPQA–Diamond, which explains why the state size matters more on these tasks. Overall, these results indicate that <math id="A5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> suffices for robust Markovian behavior in R1 models with only a modest state carryover, while Qwen3 benefits from a larger state that better matches its long–context prior on long–reasoning workloads.
However, with <math id="A5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, even R1-Distill family show similar pattern as Qwen3 (<a href="#A5.F4" title="In Appendix E Markovian State Size Ablation ‣ Appendix ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.4</span></a>).
This suggest that there is an interplay between the context size and the average response length.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06557v1/prefill_ablation.svg" id="A5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/144;" width="476" height="144"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure E.3</span>: </span><span id="A5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation of the Markovian state size <math id="A5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> at fixed per-chunk context <math id="A5.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. R1-Distill models (1.5B to 14B) achieve stable accuracy even with small carried states (<math id="A5.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\ll\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\ll\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that only modest memory is required for Markovian behavior. In contrast, Qwen3 (native 256k context) shows clear gains from larger state sizes, reflecting its long-context prior and the longer Delethink traces on AIME and LiveCodeBench tasks.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06557v1/prefill_ablation_4k.svg" id="A5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/137;" width="476" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure E.4</span>: </span><span id="A5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation of the Markovian state size <math id="A5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> at fixed per-chunk context <math id="A5.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast to <math id="A5.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, R1-Distill 7B shows gains from larger state sizes across most tasks,
whereas R1-Dstill 1.5B only benefits from larger state sizes in AIME’25 and GPQA.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Thinking Context Size Ablation</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next ablate the effect of the per-chunk thinking context size <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> on test-time scaling across all R1–Distill models (1.5B, 7B, 14B) and four benchmarks (AIME’24, AIME’25, GPQA–Diamond, LiveCodeBench).
In these experiments, the <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>–axis reports the total thinking budget (number of generated tokens).
For smaller context sizes, Delethink necessarily performs more iterations to reach the same total budget: for example, Delethink with <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> executes roughly four times as many iterations as <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to reach 128k tokens. We set the carried state to <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\mathcal{C}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\mathcal{C}/2</annotation></semantics></math> in every configuration so that only <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is varied.
This setup enables a direct comparison of how context size alone impacts performance under a fixed token budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results, shown in Figure <a href="#A5.F5" title="Figure E.5 ‣ E.1 Thinking Context Size Ablation ‣ Appendix E Markovian State Size Ablation ‣ Appendix ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.5</span></a>, emphasize the strong zero–shot Markovian behavior of R1 models. Despite not being trained for Markovian tracing, all R1 models steadily improve with increased budget and in many cases surpass the LongCoT baseline.
Most notably, for the 1.5B model the <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> configuration is highly effective: its scaling curve is nearly log–linear, and it even outperforms the <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> setting on AIME’24, AIME’25, and LiveCodeBench.
This surprising robustness highlights that smaller chunks can sometimes be more efficient for test–time scaling in smaller models.
As the model size increases to 7B and 14B, the advantage of <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> diminishes and <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically best or tied.
By contrast, <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> proves extremely limited: it rarely approaches the LongCoT baseline and only manages to catch up on the AIME tasks for the 1.5B model at very high budgets.
This consistent underperformance suggests that such a short context severely constrains Delethink’s ability to maintain stable traces.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06557v1/qwen_1B_to_14B_delethink_infer_extend.svg" id="A5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/307;" width="476" height="307"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure E.5</span>: </span><span id="A5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Thinking Context Size Ablation. Scaling behavior of R1-Distill models across AIME’24, AIME’25, GPQA-Diamond, and LiveCodeBench under varying per-hunk contexts <math id="A5.F5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{C}\in\{2\text{k},4\text{k},8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext>k</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mtext>k</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\in\{2\text{k},4\text{k},8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Accuracy is plotted against total thinking tokens, with smaller contexts requiring proportionally more iterations.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The patterns become even clearer when examining completion behavior and response lengths (Figure <a href="#A5.F6" title="Figure E.6 ‣ E.1 Thinking Context Size Ablation ‣ Appendix E Markovian State Size Ablation ‣ Appendix ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.6</span></a>).
With <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, models almost never terminate within budget, leading to dramatically lower EOS rates across tasks and scales.
Correspondingly, the average response lengths at <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are much higher than at 4k or 8k, reflecting the model’s difficulty in finalizing its reasoning.
This behavior is consistent with the limited horizon imposed by such a short per–chunk view: the model repeatedly resets positional embeddings, and the bias toward continuation overwhelms the signal to conclude.
In practice, this prevents stable Markovian progression and explains why <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> remains far below LongCoT except in a few cases (e.g., AIME at 1.5B scale).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06557v1/thinking_context_ablation_vs_eos.svg" id="A5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure E.6</span>: </span><span id="A5.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Limitation of extremely low thinking context size.
(a) EOS rate across tasks shows that under a 2k context, R1 models almost never terminate within budget, unlike 4k and 8k.
(b) On AIME’24, <math id="A5.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> produces much longer responses for all model sizes, indicating difficulty finalizing reasoning due to short local horizon and positional bias.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these results provide further evidence that R1 models exhibit strong Markovian behavior even without explicit training.
Smaller contexts can be unexpectedly effective: <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=4\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> not only scales smoothly but, for the 1.5B model, even surpasses <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> on several tasks.
At the same time, the severe degradation of <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=2\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> highlights the limits of compressing the local horizon too aggressively, as models struggle to converge their reasoning and rarely complete within budget.
Together, the ablations show that test-time Markovian scaling is a robust property of off-the-shelf R1 models, while also clarifying the practical regimes in which context size supports or hinders long-horizon reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Delethink Tracing on LongCoT checkpoints</h3>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Why Delethink Works? ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, off-the-shelf LLMs show Markovian traces under Delethink, allowing us to generate traces even from models not explicitly trained with it. This suggests that applying Delethink Tracing could boost the performance of the LongCoT-<math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> checkpoint, despite it not being trained with Delethink. We investigate this in <a href="#A6.F7" title="In Appendix F Delethink Tracing on LongCoT checkpoints ‣ Appendix ‣ The Markovian Thinker" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.7</span></a>. We find that Delethink Tracing enables the LongCoT-<math id="A6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> checkpoint to test-time scale far beyond its training limits. Its performance increases by nearly <math id="A6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\%</annotation></semantics></math>, almost as much as the gain it achieved from its entire RL training, at no additional training cost. This is a surprising phenomenon. Our hypothesis is that constraining queries to fewer than <math id="A6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\textnormal{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\textnormal{k}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens creates the illusion, for the <math id="A6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\textnormal{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\textnormal{k}</annotation></semantics></math> baseline, that it still has capacity left to continue. However, we find that when queries are kept under <math id="A6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\textnormal{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\textnormal{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, this effect vanishes, dissolving the <em id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infinite budget illusion</em>. While these results indicate that Delethink Tracing might enhance traditionally trained checkpoints, we note that such baselines require quadratically more compute to train than Delethink.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06557v1/delethink_on_longcot_checkpoint.svg" id="A6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/160;" width="429" height="160"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A6.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure F.7</span>: </span><span id="A6.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Delethink Tracing on a LongCoT–RL 24K checkpoint (AIME’25, avg@128). LongCoT plateaus near its trained budget, while Delethink (<math id="A6.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=16\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=16\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) keeps scaling; <math id="A6.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=8\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the larger gain. The shaded region marks the trained budget.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Budget Force Ablation: Does S1 work on R1?</h3>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <em id="A7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Budget-Force</em> decoding scheme inspired by the S1 “simple test-time scaling” protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We run decoding and, whenever the model <em id="A7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finalizes early</em> before using the allotted token budget, we cut the response at the first finalization marker and append a short continuation cue before regenerating from the <em id="A7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">entire</em> prompt. Concretely, for each query we first sample with temperature <math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6</annotation></semantics></math>, top-<math id="A7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> under a 32k token budget. If the model emits any of <code id="A7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;/think&gt;</code>, <code id="A7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">**Final Answer**</code>, or <code id="A7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{</code> (or EOS) before the budget is consumed, we trim at the earliest occurrence, append the literal cue <code id="A7.p1.1.7" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">Wait\n</code>, and re-issue a generation from query + cut_response. We repeat this micro-forcing until the round budget is exhausted or a continuation cap (20) is reached. To avoid distributional drift, we do not alter model decoding settings mid-run; the only intervention is the prompt-level cut-and-continue. At the end, if the model still has not emitted EOS after fully using the budget, we add a compact finalization hint (<code id="A7.p1.1.8" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">**Final Answer**\n</code>) and request a short natural continuation. All models identical sampling parameters across conditions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A7.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table G.2</span>: </span><span id="A7.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy (avg@128, %) for LongCoT, Budget-Force, and Delethink across benchmarks, using identical sampling and each method’s native scaling protocol.</span></figcaption>
